    "body": "`ChainstateManager` creates and owns its `BlockManager`, which means block storage can't exist without chainstate management. #35627 discussed moving that ownership out of `ChainstateManager`, and the kernel needs it to configure, read and prune block storage on its own.\n\n`NodeContext` and the kernel now create the `BlockManager` first, pass it to `ChainstateManager` by reference and destroy `ChainstateManager` first.\n\nThe first commit makes the kernel's `ChainMan` destroy its `ChainstateManager` explicitly. On master `ChainMan` declares its context last, which destroys the context before the `ChainstateManager` that refers into it. Nothing reads through those references during teardown, so this changes no behavior, but the order no longer depends on how the members are declared.\n\nThe last commit removes `ChainstateLoadOptions::prune`. It duplicated the `BlockManager`'s prune mode, and the kernel never set it. Once the kernel can prune (#36856), that would make it refuse to reopen a data directory it had pruned.\n\nThe only behavior change to my knowledge is startup log order, because the block tree database opens before the script verification threads start.\n\nAn earlier version of this PR also moved `ImportBlocks` and replaced `BlockManager`'s friend declarations. I've taken them out as they're not relevant to this, but I think together better suited for a change that removes the `node/blockstorage -> validation` circular dependency.\n\nThe kernel side is #36854.",
